<script>
import askClaudeSource from 'crm/src/assistant/askClaude.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import askClaudeCodeSource from 'crm/src/assistant/askClaudeCode.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import ClaudeApiRequest from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeApiRequest.abide'
import claudeApiRequestSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeApiRequest.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import claudeApiRequestTestSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeApiRequest.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import ClaudeCodeBridge from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeCodeBridge.abide'
import claudeCodeBridgeSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeCodeBridge.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import claudeCodeBridgeTestSource from 'crm/src/assistant/lib/ClaudeCodeBridge.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import messagesSource from 'crm/src/assistant/v1/messages.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`provider.claudeApi` sends the request body you wrote">
        <Example
            id="claude-api"
            app="crm"
            title="`provider.claudeApi(config)` — the request body you wrote, sent as written"
            summary="The assistant on Anthropic's API, its baseURL pointed at a stand-in in the CRM that answers with the body it was sent. Ask, and read the request."
            files={{
                'src/assistant/lib/ClaudeApiRequest.abide': claudeApiRequestSource,
                'src/assistant/askClaude.handler.ts': askClaudeSource,
                'src/assistant/v1/messages.handler.ts': messagesSource,
                'src/assistant/lib/ClaudeApiRequest.test.ts': claudeApiRequestTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ClaudeApiRequest />
        </Example>

        <p><code>provider.claudeApi</code> takes a Messages API request body as Anthropic documents it,
            with the client's <code>apiKey</code> and <code>baseURL</code> beside it. Its type is
            <code>ClaudeApiConfig</code>. abide adds <code>messages</code> and <code>stream</code>, and
            every other field is sent as you wrote it.</p>

        <p>Leave <code>baseURL</code> out and the request goes to Anthropic. <code>provider.claudeApi</code>
            reads no key from the environment, so pass <code>apiKey</code> from wherever the CRM keeps
            its secrets.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`provider.claudeCode` runs on the visitor's own Claude Code">
        <Example
            id="claude-code"
            app="crm"
            title="`provider.claudeCode(config)` — a turn on the reader's own Claude Code"
            summary="The assistant on whatever Claude Code is paired with this browser. Ask first and it is refused; pair with a click or run the command in a terminal, and ask again."
            files={{
                'src/assistant/lib/ClaudeCodeBridge.abide': claudeCodeBridgeSource,
                'src/assistant/askClaudeCode.handler.ts': askClaudeCodeSource,
                'src/assistant/lib/ClaudeCodeBridge.test.ts': claudeCodeBridgeTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ClaudeCodeBridge />
        </Example>

        <p><code>provider.claudeCode</code> sends the conversation to Claude Code on the asker's machine,
            on their own sign-in and plan. Its config, <code>ClaudeCodeConfig</code>, is Claude Code's
            own option names, each passed as its flag: <code>model</code>, <code>fallbackModel</code>,
            <code>systemPrompt</code>, <code>appendSystemPrompt</code>, <code>effort</code>,
            <code>maxBudgetUsd</code>, <code>betas</code> and <code>disallowedTools</code>. Permissions,
            directories and settings are not the site's to set.</p>

        <p><code>tools</code> asks for tools of the visitor's machine, and the visitor decides.
            Name a built-in tool, such as <code>WebSearch</code>, or a tool of an MCP server the
            visitor configured, as Claude Code names it: <code>mcp__calendar__meetings</code>, or
            <code>mcp__calendar</code> for all of that server's. A tool runs only where they granted
            it after the command's <code>--</code>, and the bridge tells them about any it was asked
            for and not given. Anything else after <code>--</code> goes to Claude Code as written,
            and overrides what the site set.</p>

        <Code code={`bunx @abide/bridge <site> <code> -- --tools WebSearch,mcp__calendar__meetings`} />

        <p>The site names tools, never servers: a server is a command that runs on the visitor's
            machine. The bridge finds it in their own Claude Code configuration, the user scope
            <code>claude mcp add --scope user</code> writes, or in each <code>--mcp-config</code>
            they pass instead. It reads them again at every turn, so a server added mid-visit is
            used at the next question, and a turn gets only the servers of the tools it was
            given.</p>

        <p>Claude Code runs in its auto mode, so a granted tool is not approved just because it was
            granted. Auto mode approves what it judges safe, and anything it would ask the visitor
            about arrives on the page as a <code>permission</code> event, naming the tool and its
            input. The page answers with <code>provider.claudeCode.permit(id, allow)</code> from a
            handler of its own. Only the browser the bridge is paired with can answer, and a prompt
            nobody answers is denied after two minutes. The site's own tools are never asked
            about.</p>

        <p>A visitor who wants the site to have everything their Claude Code has runs the bridge
            with <code>--full</code>: every built-in tool, every MCP server and every setting it
            would read on its own, still in an empty directory and still in auto mode. The native
            app's own bridge always runs this way.</p>

        <Code code={`bunx @abide/bridge <site> <code> --full`} />

        <p>A site can ask for it. Pass <code>full: true</code> to
            <code>provider.claudeCode.bridge</code> or <code>provider.claudeCode.bridgeCommand</code>,
            or turn on <code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_FULL</code> for <code>pairing()</code>, and the command
            carries <code>--full</code> and the link asks for full access. Opening that link names no
            tool: it says the site is requesting full access to the visitor's Claude Code, and offers
            Allow Full Access, Only Site Tools and Don't Allow. Only Site Tools pairs with the site's
            own tools and none of the machine's.</p>

        <Code code={`onConfig(() => ({ ABIDE_BRIDGE_FULL: true }))`} />

        <p>The question can say why, in the app's own words. Pass <code>note</code> beside
            <code>tools</code> or <code>full</code>, or set <code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_NOTE</code> for
            <code>pairing()</code>. The note travels with the pairing's code, never in its link, and
            the question shows it under its headline as one line from the app, cut at 200
            characters. Everything else in the question is the bridge's own.</p>

        <Code code={`onConfig(() => ({ ABIDE_BRIDGE_NOTE: 'So it can file your deals.' }))`} />

        <p><code>bunx @abide/bridge</code> runs <code>abide-bridge &lt;site&gt; &lt;code&gt;</code>.
            <code>--claude &lt;path&gt;</code> names the Claude Code to run where
            <code>claude</code> is not on the <code>PATH</code>, and each
            <code>--env NAME=VALUE</code> sets a variable for it, such as a proxy or an API key of
            the visitor's own.</p>

        <p>Pass the same <code>tools</code> to <code>provider.claudeCode.bridge</code> or
            <code>provider.claudeCode.bridgeCommand</code> and the command it mints carries that grant, so a
            visitor who runs it as shown gives the tools. One minted without them grants none.</p>

        <p>The visitor pairs once per visit by running a command, which dials out to the site.
            Asking with nothing paired is refused 409. Every status carries a command, paired or
            not, and a bridge paired with it takes the place of the one paired, so a page can offer
            a reconnect without waiting for the old bridge to go.</p>

        <p>Each status also carries <code>missing</code>: the tools the pairing asked for that
            the paired bridge reports it cannot give, because the visitor did not grant one or has
            no server for it. Show the visitor what to add from it.</p>

        <p>On macOS, Linux and Windows the visitor can pair with a click instead. Each status
            carries a <code>link</code> beside its command, the same pairing in the
            <code>abide-bridge</code> scheme, and a visitor who has run this once opens it from the
            page:</p>

        <Code code={`bunx @abide/bridge install`} />

        <p><code>pairing()</code> is that click, and the page needs no handler of its own for it.
            It reads this browser's status from <code>/__abide/pairing</code>, which abide mounts
            and keeps off MCP and OpenAPI, and follows it over the socket the page already holds,
            so <code>status</code> is always the latest and no connection waits on it. It is
            <code>undefined</code> until the first status lands, which is when the card says
            Connecting, and <code>await status</code> waits for that first one. A read that
            failed is on <code>status.error()</code>, the way a refused write is, and reading
            <code>status</code> still answers what it held, so nothing throws. A server render
            holds <code>undefined</code> and does not wait.
            <code>start</code> opens its link. A browser cannot tell whether a link opened anything,
            but the app opening it blurs the page, so a click that blurs nothing and pairs nothing
            within 2.5 seconds sets <code>step</code> to <code>install</code>.
            <code>copyInstall</code> copies the install command and holds <code>copied</code> for a
            moment. Unmounting the component stops the read and the timers. A
            <code>pairing()</code> built anywhere else, in a click handler, runs until its
            <code>stop</code>, which <code>using</code> calls too.</p>

        <p>The server decides what that pairing grants, never the page. Set
            <code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_TOOLS</code> to the same tools your <code>provider.claudeCode</code> turns
            ask for, separated by commas, in an <code>onConfig</code> default or the environment.
            Unset, it grants none, and a tool a request names is ignored.</p>

        <Code code={`onConfig(() => ({ ABIDE_BRIDGE_TOOLS: 'WebSearch,mcp__calendar__meetings' }))`} />

        <p>What the visitor runs can carry options of the site's own, per person. Each
            <code>onPairing</code> answers the Claude Code options for the request asking, so it can
            read <code>principal</code>, and they follow the <code>--</code> of the command and of
            the install command, quoted for a shell. The link carries none, and a request names
            none.</p>

        <Code code={`onPairing(() => ['--mcp-config', \`/configs/\${principal.resolved().id}.json\`])`} />

        <p>That registers a handler for the scheme under the visitor's own home: a small app in
            Application Support on macOS, a desktop entry on Linux, the visitor's own registry
            classes on Windows. <code>abide-bridge install</code> takes the same
            <code>--full</code>, <code>--claude</code>, <code>--env</code> and options after
            <code>--</code> as the command, and keeps them: every link it opens runs Claude Code with
            them, and no link can set any of them. A link's ask for full access runs only where the
            visitor allows it. Opening a link asks first. The question names the site and the tools it
            asks for, any tool whose server they lack, and the options and variable names install
            kept, or says the site asks for full access, or that it gets it where install was run
            <code>--full</code>. Then it runs the bridge release the link names, as the command would. A Linux desktop with neither
            <code>zenity</code> nor <code>kdialog</code> cannot ask, so it pairs nothing from a
            link.</p>

        <p>Allow is kept. A link only proves the site minted its code, never that this visitor
            asked for it, so the bridge keeps the answer for the site and for whom the code was
            minted: the signed-in member, or the browser when nobody is. A later link pairs unasked
            only where its code was minted for the same one and asks for no new tool, and for full
            access only where they answered that ask before, as they answered it. A site allowed full
            access pairs unasked whatever it asks for. A code someone else minted and put in front of
            them is asked about, and so is every link from a site that names nobody.
            <code>abide-bridge forget</code> asks again for one site, or every one, and
            <code>abide-bridge uninstall</code> takes away everything install wrote.</p>

        <p>A bridge that loses the site says so once, and not again until the site takes a report.
            A turn whose report the site did not take is ended, and reported failed.</p>

        <p>The tools the conversation calls run as the visitor, on the sign-in their ask carried,
            so a tool reads <code>principal</code> as any handler does. The pairing belongs to that
            sign-in too. If someone else signs in at the same browser, their asks are refused 409
            and the status offers them a command of their own, so nobody's turn runs on another
            member's Claude Code.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>pairing</code></td><td>This browser's pairing in a page: <code>status</code>, <code>step</code> and <code>copied</code>, with <code>start</code> to pair with a click, <code>copyInstall</code> to copy the install command and <code>stop</code> to let it go.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>/__abide/pairing</code></td><td>Where <code>pairing</code> reads this browser's status, followed over the socket. On no generated surface.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_TOOLS</code></td><td>The tools <code>/__abide/pairing</code> grants, separated by commas. Unset, none.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_FULL</code></td><td>Whether <code>/__abide/pairing</code> asks for full access. Unset, it does not.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_BRIDGE_NOTE</code></td><td>The app's note on why it asks, shown in the question a pairing link asks. Unset, none.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>onPairing</code></td><td>The options every pairing's command and install command carry after <code>--</code>, for the person asking.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>provider.claudeCode.bridge</code></td><td>Streams this browser's pairing: unpaired, then paired, each with the command to run and the link to open.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>BridgeStatus</code></td><td>One status in that stream, <code>{'{ paired, missing, command, link, install }'}</code>. <code>install</code> is the command that installs what opens the link, at the release the command runs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>provider.claudeCode.permit</code></td><td>Answers a <code>permission</code> event the paired bridge is waiting on, allowing the call or not.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>provider.claudeCode.bridgeCommand</code></td><td>Mints the command and the link alone, for a page that wants them without the stream. Its code expires unused after ten minutes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>/__abide/bridge</code></td><td>Where the visitor's bridge connects. It opens the first time a command is minted.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
